<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conrad III of Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1093">1093</a>–<a href="/years/1152">1152</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Conrad III was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1138 to 1152, the first ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen dynasty</a>. His election began the long rivalry between the Hohenstaufen and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welfs</a>. He led the German army on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>, which was mauled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in 1147 and failed before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1148. He was never crowned emperor, and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-of-germany.jpg" alt="Conrad III of Germany" width="320" height="708" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konrad_III_Miniatur_13_Jahrhundert.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Conrad was the son of Duke Frederick I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and Agnes, a daughter of Emperor Henry IV. After his father's death in 1105 his elder brother Frederick II inherited Swabia. In 1116 their uncle, Emperor Henry V, made Conrad Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>. When Henry V died in 1125, Conrad unsuccessfully backed Frederick for the kingship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Frederick was banned and Conrad lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>. With the support of the imperial cities, Swabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> he was elected anti-king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in December 1127, was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a> but achieved little there, and in 1135 acknowledged Lothair III as emperor. After Lothair died in December 1137, Conrad was elected king at Coblenz on 7 March 1138 and crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> six days later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-proud">Henry the Proud</a>, the most powerful prince in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, refused to accept this, and Conrad stripped him of his lands. The civil war that followed is considered the first act of the struggle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelphs-and-ghibellines">Guelphs and Ghibellines</a>. A peace was made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in May 1142.</p><p>In 1146 Conrad heard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> preach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> at Speyer and joined Louis VII. He had his son Henry Berengar elected king before leaving. His army of about 20,000 men crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> rather than taking the coast, and on 25 October 1147 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> defeated it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a>, killing or capturing most of the foot soldiers. Conrad fell ill at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, recovered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and later took part in the failed Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1150 Conrad and Henry Berengar defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welf-vi">Welf VI</a> and his son at Flochberg. Henry Berengar died that year, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welfs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> made peace in 1152. Conrad was never crowned emperor and styled himself King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> until his death on 15 February 1152. According to the account of his last hours, he named his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> as successor rather than his six-year-old son Frederick. Barbarossa was elected king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> a few weeks later, and the son received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Conrad III of Germany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sultanate repeatedly defeated passing crusaders: Kilij Arslan I destroyed the expeditions of 1101, and Mesud I’s forces routed the German army of <b>Conrad III</b> near Dorylaeum during the Second Crusade in 1147.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Second Crusade, the armies of Louis VII of France and <b>Conrad III of Germany</b>, with that of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, laid siege to Damascus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-24">24 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The siege of Damascus by the armies of Louis VII of France, <b>Conrad III of Germany</b> and the Kingdom of Jerusalem failed after four days.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-28">28 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Dukes were often royal relatives, and from 1079 the office went to the Staufer family, who became emperors with <b>Conrad III</b> and Frederick Barbarossa.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-swabia">Duchy of Swabia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1139 King <b>Conrad III</b> placed it under the Archbishop of Trier, beginning centuries of dispute over its status.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-saint-maximin">Imperial Abbey of Saint Maximin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.89,10.89&amp;year=1131" aria-label="Bamberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Conrad III of Germany"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5596 5811h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5596 5811h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.89,10.89&amp;year=1131">What was here in 1131? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
